<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>ArtaQuantum</title><subtitle>Daily notes on quantum</subtitle><link href="https://artaquantum.github.io/"/><link rel="self" href="https://artaquantum.github.io/atom.xml"/><id>https://artaquantum.github.io/</id><updated>2026-09-23T00:00:00Z</updated><author><name>ArtaQuantum</name></author><entry><title>First note</title><link href="https://artaquantum.github.io/notes/2026-09-23-first-note.html"/><id>https://artaquantum.github.io/notes/2026-09-23-first-note.html</id><updated>2026-09-23T00:00:00Z</updated><content type="html">&lt;p&gt;Short daily notes on quantum information and computation: a result worked through, a derivation checked, a computation that surprised me.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Math is written in LaTeX. Inline, the Bell state &lt;span class=&quot;arithmatex&quot;&gt;\(|\Phi^+\rangle = (|00\rangle + |11\rangle)/\sqrt{2}\)&lt;/span&gt;; on its own line, its reduced state on either qubit is maximally mixed:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;arithmatex&quot;&gt;\[
\rho_A = \operatorname{Tr}_B |\Phi^+\rangle\langle\Phi^+| = \tfrac{1}{2}\,I.
\]&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Code is shown with its language named on the fence:&lt;/p&gt;
